Feature: The sign-in cookie sealing secret is generated by infrastructure

  The Studio seals its sign-in cookies with a random secret kept in SSM Parameter Store. Infrastructure generates it
  once, as a SecureString, and nothing else ever creates or sees it: it is in no template, output or stack event, a
  redeploy never regenerates it, and deleting the stack leaves it in place. The server finds it by a name the deployed
  backend publishes, so there is no operator step. Nothing here calls AWS: the stack is synthesized and the creating
  function is run against a fake SSM client.

  Rule: The secret is a SecureString created once

    Scenario: The creating function stores a SecureString and never overwrites
      Given a fresh SSM Parameter Store
      When the sealing parameter is created
      Then the parameter is a SecureString
      And the stored value is at least 32 characters
      And the creating function returned only the parameter name

    Scenario: A redeploy does not regenerate or overwrite the secret
      Given a fresh SSM Parameter Store
      And the sealing parameter is created
      When the stack is updated
      Then the stored value is unchanged
      And the create request said not to overwrite

    Scenario: A parameter that already exists is adopted as it is
      Given an SSM Parameter Store that already holds the sealing parameter
      When the sealing parameter is created
      Then the stored value is unchanged

    Scenario: Deleting the stack does not touch the parameter
      Given a fresh SSM Parameter Store
      And the sealing parameter is created
      When the stack is deleted
      Then the parameter still exists with the same value

    Scenario: Any other failure is reported without the value
      Given an SSM Parameter Store that refuses the write
      When the sealing parameter is created
      Then the creation fails with an error that does not contain the stored value

  Rule: The template holds no secret and keeps the parameter

    Scenario: The synthesized stack retains the parameter on delete and replace
      Given the sealing stack is synthesized for the "main" branch
      Then the custom resource is retained on delete and on replacement

    Scenario: The template and outputs contain no secret value
      Given the sealing stack is synthesized for the "main" branch
      Then the template has no value property for the parameter
      And the outputs are only the parameter name and its ARN

    Scenario: The secret is not in Secrets Manager
      Given the sealing stack is synthesized for the "main" branch
      Then the template has no Secrets Manager resource
      And the sealing code never mentions Secrets Manager

    Scenario: The creating role can only write that one parameter
      Given the sealing stack is synthesized for the "main" branch
      Then the only SSM permission is PutParameter on the sealing parameter

  Rule: The server finds the secret by a name from the deployment

    Scenario Outline: Each branch gets its own parameter
      Given the deployment branch is "<branch>"
      Then the sealing parameter name is "<name>"

      Examples:
        | branch      | name                                            |
        | main        | /babulus/studio/main/prospectus-session-sealing |
        | feat/new_ui | /babulus/studio/feat-new-ui/prospectus-session-sealing |
        |             | /babulus/studio/sandbox/prospectus-session-sealing |

    Scenario: The server reads the parameter named in the backend outputs
      Given the backend outputs name the sealing parameter
      And no sealing parameter name is set in the environment
      When the sign-in runtime is resolved
      Then the secret was read from the parameter named in the outputs

    Scenario: A short sealing secret is refused
      Given the backend outputs name the sealing parameter
      And the stored sealing secret is too short
      When the sign-in runtime is resolved
      Then the sign-in runtime is unavailable
